DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmrreemmaapp()  expands  (or shrinks) an existing memory mapping, potentially
       moving it at the same time (controlled by the _f_l_a_g_s  argument  and  the
       available virtual address space).

       Mappings  can also simply be moved (without any resizing) by specifying
       equal _o_l_d___s_i_z_e and _n_e_w___s_i_z_e and using the MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD  flag  (see  be‐
       low).  Since Linux 6.17, while _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s must be mapped, _o_l_d___s_i_z_e may
       span  multiple mappings including unmapped areas between them when per‐
       forming a simple move.  The MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP flag may  also  be  speci‐
       fied.

       Similarly,  if the operation performs a shrink, that is, if _o_l_d___s_i_z_e is
       greater than _n_e_w___s_i_z_e, then _o_l_d___s_i_z_e may also span  multiple  mappings,
       which  do  not  have  to be adjacent to one another.  If this shrink is
       performed in‐place, that is, neither MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD, nor MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP
       are specified, _n_e_w___s_i_z_e may also span multiple VMAs.  However,  if  the
       range is moved, then _n_e_w___s_i_z_e must span only a single mapping.

       If  the  operation  is  neither  a MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD move nor a shrink, then
       _o_l_d___s_i_z_e must span only a single mapping.

       _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s is the old address of the first virtual memory  block  that
       you  want to expand, shrink, and/or move.  Note that _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s has to
       be page aligned.  _o_l_d___s_i_z_e is the size of the range containing  virtual
       memory blocks to be manipulated.  _n_e_w___s_i_z_e is the requested size of the
       virtual  memory  blocks  after the resize.  An optional fifth argument,
       _n_e_w___a_d_d_r_e_s_s, may be provided; see the description of  MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD  be‐
       low.

       If the value of _o_l_d___s_i_z_e is zero, and _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s refers to a shareable
       mapping  (see  the description of MMAAPP__SSHHAARREEDD in mmmmaapp(2)), then mmrreemmaapp()
       will create a new mapping of the same pages.  _n_e_w___s_i_z_e will be the size
       of the new mapping and the location of the new mapping may be specified
       with _n_e_w___a_d_d_r_e_s_s; see the description of MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD below.  If a  new
       mapping is requested via this method, then the MMRREEMMAAPP__MMAAYYMMOOVVEE flag must
       also be specified.

       The _f_l_a_g_s bit‐mask argument may be 0, or include the following flags:

       MMRREEMMAAPP__MMAAYYMMOOVVEE
              By default, if there is not sufficient space to expand a mapping
              at  its  current location, then mmrreemmaapp() fails.  If this flag is
              specified, then the kernel is permitted to relocate the  mapping
              to a new virtual address, if necessary.  If the mapping is relo‐
              cated,  then absolute pointers into the old mapping location be‐
              come invalid (offsets relative to the starting  address  of  the
              mapping should be employed).

       MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD (since Linux 2.3.31)
              This  flag  serves  a  similar  purpose to the MMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD flag of
              mmmmaapp(2).  If this flag is specified,  then  mmrreemmaapp()  accepts  a
              fifth  argument,  _v_o_i_d _*_n_e_w___a_d_d_r_e_s_s,  which  specifies  a  page‐
              aligned address to which the mapping must be moved.  Any  previ‐
              ous  mapping  at  the address range specified by _n_e_w___a_d_d_r_e_s_s and
              _n_e_w___s_i_z_e is unmapped.

              If MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD is specified, then MMRREEMMAAPP__MMAAYYMMOOVVEE must  also  be
              specified.

              Since   Linux  6.17,  if  _o_l_d___s_i_z_e  is  equal  to  _n_e_w___s_i_z_e  and
              MMRREEMMAAPP__FFIIXXEEDD is specified, then _o_l_d___s_i_z_e  may  span  beyond  the
              mapping  in  which  _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s resides.  In this case, gaps be‐
              tween mappings in the original range are maintained in  the  new
              range.   The  whole  operation is performed atomically unless an
              error arises, in which case the operation may be partially  com‐
              pleted, that is, some mappings may be moved and others not.

              Moving  multiple  mappings is not permitted if any of those map‐
              pings have either been registered with  uusseerrffaauullttffdd(2),,  or  map
              drivers that specify their own custom address mapping logic.

       MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP (since Linux 5.7)
              This  flag,  which  must be used in conjunction with MMRREEMMAAPP__MMAAYY‐‐
              MMOOVVEE, remaps mappings to a new address but does not  unmap  them
              from their original address.

              The  MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP  flag  can be used only with mappings that
              are not VVMM__DDOONNTTEEXXPPAANNDD or VVMM__MMIIXXEEDDMMAAPP.  Before  Linux  5.13,  the
              MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP  flag could be used only with private anonymous
              mappings (see the description of MMAAPP__PPRRIIVVAATTEE  and  MMAAPP__AANNOONNYYMMOOUUSS
              in mmmmaapp(2)).

              After  completion,  any access to the range specified by _o_l_d___a_d_‐
              _d_r_e_s_s and _o_l_d___s_i_z_e will result in a page fault.  The page  fault
              will be handled by a uusseerrffaauullttffdd(2) handler if the address is in
              a  range  previously registered with uusseerrffaauullttffdd(2).  Otherwise,
              the kernel allocates a zero‐filled page to handle the fault.

              The MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP flag may be used to atomically move a  map‐
              ping while leaving the source mapped.  See NOTES for some possi‐
              ble applications of MMRREEMMAAPP__DDOONNTTUUNNMMAAPP.

       If the memory segments specified by _o_l_d___a_d_d_r_e_s_s and _o_l_d___s_i_z_e are locked
       (using mmlloocckk(2) or similar), then this lock is maintained when the seg‐
       ments  are  resized  and/or relocated.  As a consequence, the amount of
       memory locked by the process may change.

